        "hasScanResult": true,
        "sha256hash": "bb865c41802f43c04633f426fc52818206da60c57a65f23a1f2ae958b58d4701",
        "virustotalUrl": "https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/bb865c41802f43c04633f426fc52818206da60c57a65f23a1f2ae958b58d4701",
        "scanners": {
          "vt": null,
          "skillspector": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "score": 100,
            "severity": "CRITICAL",
            "recommendation": "DO_NOT_INSTALL",
            "issueCount": 33,
            "checkedAt": 1781035218821
          },
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "high",
            "summary": "This appears to be a legitimate full-stack website scaffold, but it needs Review because its deployable auth, payment, analytics, and migration code contains unsafe defaults and misleading security claims.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The stated purpose covers full-stack website generation with auth, payments, AI chat, admin, analytics, and deployment, so the broad capability is mostly coherent; however, artifact code contradicts several security claims, including no-op JWT middleware, a plaintext-password login path, and a payment helper that accepts parsed JSON as valid.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Triggers are broad and deployment/migration capabilities are powerful, but SKILL.md includes explicit user-confirmation checkpoints before generation, deployment, payment activation, production secrets, database migration, cron enablement, and rollback.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "No postinstall or automatically executed package scripts were found. The package exposes manual scripts for build, test, publish, init, migration, and deployment guidance; README demo links include token-like query parameters, which is poor secret hygiene.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Secret requirements for JWT, AI, payment, and database access are expected for the stated stack, but the generated production-adjacent code handles high-impact auth/payment/admin paths with incomplete enforcement and privacy controls.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill creates persistent sessions, refresh-token metadata, analytics identifiers/events, AI chat history, audit/order records, and cron-style order mutations. These are purpose-aligned, but analytics initializes automatically and several privileged flows lack robust consent, authorization, or fail-closed behavior.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
              }
            ],
            "guidance": "Install only if you will treat this as a review-required scaffold, not production-ready code. Before deploying, replace placeholder payment verification with provider-approved signature checks, remove tokenized demo links, disable or gate analytics behind consent, fix auth middleware and password verification, rotate/change demo credentials, and approve migrations, cron jobs, payment activation, and production secrets only after reviewing the generated target environment.",
            "findings": "[LP3] expected: The skill declares secret env vars in SKILL.md and uses deployment/database/payment capabilities that fit a full-stack scaffold. The concern is not hidden capability, but the high-impact scope and weak implementation safeguards.\n[TP4] expected: Auth, payments, AI chat, admin, analytics, notifications, and multi-tenant features are broadly disclosed in the artifacts, not silently injected. The breadth still increases review risk because users may expect a simple website generator.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: README.md contains live demo URLs with eo_token and eo_time query parameters. This is not needed for a public skill and normalizes token-bearing links in documentation.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The trigger phrases include very general website-building requests. Because this skill can scaffold deployable infrastructure and request secrets, accidental invocation is a real concern despite later confirmation checkpoints.\n[SQP-2] expected: Deployment and migration are expected for this skill, and SKILL.md includes a confirmation table. Users should still review target environment, database impact, and costs before approving those steps.\n[SDI-1] expected: Notification hooks and cron/order automation are documented as optional or operational modules. They are not hidden, but enabling them can send messages or mutate business state and should require explicit configuration.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: Analytics is documented, but client/src/app.js calls initAnalytics automatically and client/src/utils/analytics.js collects stable identifiers, URL/referrer, language, screen width, and commerce events without visible consent gating in the code reviewed.\n[SDI-4] unexpected: Several auth and payment implementations are materially unsafe for a deployable e-commerce scaffold: edge-functions/_middleware.js does not enforce JWT checks, edge-functions/api/auth/login.js can compare plaintext passwords, and packages/payment/src/index.js returns valid=true for any parseable JSON callback.\n[SDI-4] unexpected: Additional reviewed code supports the concern: logout can delete a client-supplied session ID under a default tenant fallback, seed.sql documents predictable demo credentials, and payment/reference examples contain broken or inconsistent signature-verification guidance.\n[SQP-2] expected: AI chat history persistence is coherent with an AI assistant scaffold and the code sets a 24-hour TTL, but users need a clear privacy notice before deploying chat features that store user conversations.",
            "agenticRiskFindings": null,
            "riskSummary": null,
            "model": "gpt-5.5",
            "checkedAt": 1781035315057
          }
        }
      }
